WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.473
Hello, Lattice.

2
00:00:02.473 --> 00:00:07.077
In this episode you register Lattice
on a silo, resolve a tree by name,

3
00:00:07.077 --> 00:00:09.152
and write and read typed values.

4
00:00:10.152 --> 00:00:13.226
Start with the package,
Orleans.Lattice, from NuGet.

5
00:00:13.576 --> 00:00:15.203
Then register it on the silo.

6
00:00:15.203 --> 00:00:18.401
One call registers the grain catalogue,
the grain storage

7
00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:22.047
that its callback supplies,
and the log that records every write.

8
00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:26.901
Here the grain storage is in memory,
and so, by default, is the log.

9
00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:29.087
That suits development and tests.

10
00:00:29.437 --> 00:00:31.276
In production, make both durable:

11
00:00:31.276 --> 00:00:34.563
the grain storage
that holds the tree's state, and the log.

12
00:00:34.563 --> 00:00:37.071
Going durable,
later on this path, shows how.

13
00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:42.691
Elsewhere, on a client or inside a grain,
resolve a tree by name:

14
00:00:42.691 --> 00:00:46.402
ask the grain factory for an ILattice,
with the tree's name.

15
00:00:46.752 --> 00:00:48.406
Resolution is idempotent.

16
00:00:48.406 --> 00:00:51.384
The same name always
routes to the same tree.

17
00:00:52.984 --> 00:00:56.553
At the core, keys are strings
and values are byte arrays.

18
00:00:56.553 --> 00:00:59.246
But the typed extensions serialize
for you,

19
00:00:59.246 --> 00:01:02.252
so application code rarely
touches a byte array.

20
00:01:02.602 --> 00:01:05.985
Set a record under a key,
and get it back as the same type.

21
00:01:06.335 --> 00:01:09.262
To choose your own format,
pass a serializer of your own.

22
00:01:09.262 --> 00:01:12.241
Or write the raw bytes,
when you want to own the encoding.

23
00:01:13.841 --> 00:01:17.759
Reading a key that is absent,
or deleted, returns null.

24
00:01:18.109 --> 00:01:22.088
A delete tombstones the key,
and returns whether it was live.

25
00:01:22.438 --> 00:01:25.991
And get or set writes only
when the key is absent, or deleted.

26
00:01:25.991 --> 00:01:28.455
It returns the value
that is already there,

27
00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:32.295
or null when it wrote, with no race
between the read and the write.

28
00:01:32.645 --> 00:01:36.284
And a scan streams keys back
in strict lexicographic order.

29
00:01:36.284 --> 00:01:38.443
Scans have an episode of their own.

30
00:01:40.443 --> 00:01:42.753
Next on Build: Values that merge.

31
00:01:42.753 --> 00:01:46.812
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.

32
00:01:47.162 --> 00:01:49.851
The Quick start has the setup
and the typed code.

33
00:01:49.851 --> 00:01:53.913
The API reference has the rest,
and it is the contract for each operation:

34
00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.834
what it returns and what it throws.
